\subsection*{mlpack: an explicit preprocessing contract}
The inspected mlpack imputer is an in-place strategy wrapper. It checks the dimension against the declared matrix orientation, then invokes mean, median, custom replacement or listwise deletion. The CLI binding applies the chosen strategy to one feature or, when the dimension is unspecified, to every feature. In the default column-major interpretation, a feature is a matrix row and observations are columns. A bank adapter must preserve that convention or perform an explicit conversion; a transposed table can produce plausible numbers for the wrong entities. Preserve the original data and observation mask before a routine mutates the matrix. The upstream tests demonstrate alternative orientations and sentinel replacement, but they are retained source evidence rather than locally executed qualification.~\citep{exec25_ctm_mlpack}

The sentinel is part of the scientific data contract. If genuine observed values are $(0,2)$, their mean is one. Declaring zero to represent missingness removes real information and makes the surviving mean two. Conversely, treating an absent field as a real zero changes the distribution. The same issue affects listwise deletion: it changes the analyzed population and therefore the causal target unless the selection assumptions justify the change. For a binary variable with prevalence $1/2$, observation probabilities $P(R=1\mid Y=1)=3/4$ and $P(R=1\mid Y=0)=1/4$ imply
\[
